00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:35.020
AMY BANZAERT: So let's do
a really fast estimation

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:42.100
to just make sure you're
up to speed on solar facts.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.020
So how big a solar
panel do you need

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:46.360
to power a 100-Watt
incandescent light bulb?

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:57.608
Does anyone remember
the flux, solar flux?

00:00:57.608 --> 00:00:59.902
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE].

00:00:59.902 --> 00:01:02.360
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, 1,000 Watts
per meter squared, exactly.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:05.060
And efficiency?

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:06.500
STUDENT: 20.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.870
AMY BANZAERT: 20%, 10%, 20%.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:12.860
So to make it really easy
to math, lets go with 10.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:16.460
So that would be 100
Watts per meter squared,

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:17.960
and we have a 100 light bulb.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:20.570
So assuming there's no
other inefficiency losses,

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:23.270
and we're just going directly
from solar panel to light bulb

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:28.280
for lighting in the middle of
the day-- very common need--

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:32.120
that would be a 1 meter panel,
or 1 meter squared panel.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:37.970
So I think the solar
options that we

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:40.090
covered last week are Sotus.

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:42.230
And I didn't have
time before class,

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:46.380
but I'll try to hunt
one down during the lab,

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:49.940
an example of a [? Sotus ?]
bag that a student invented.

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:52.760
That's kind of cool to see.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:55.340
Then there's solar
thermal and PV.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:58.580
And so there was a confusion
about the difference

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:01.100
between solar thermal and PV.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:06.440
So fundamentally,
solar thermal is just

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:09.740
using the greenhouse
effect to get something hot

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:15.440
and use that heat, as opposed
to PV, stands for photovoltaics.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:21.530
And that means you're taking
the photons from the sun,

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:25.130
and converting that using a
semiconductor material that

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:30.800
has a photovoltaic behavior
to get an electric current out

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:32.330
from those photons.

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:37.430
So those are quite distinct--
heat versus electricity.

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:39.260
And so solar
thermal is typically

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:41.150
used for things
like heating water

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:43.172
because it's really
easy to heat the water,

00:02:43.172 --> 00:02:45.380
well, relatively easy to
heat the water with the sun.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:47.600
And then it's just direct.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:53.100
So here's an example
of you have your panel,

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.910
which is not a photovoltaics
panel, but just

00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:59.690
a panel where you have a
volume of water that's probably

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:02.480
in a box that has
black behind it

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.940
so you're maximizing
your solar radiation.

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:06.980
Your cold water goes in.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:08.210
The water heats up.

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:10.940
Your hot water comes out, and
it goes into your hot water

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:11.850
storage tank.

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:14.270
There are, of course, many
ways to make this much more

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:21.020
complicated so that you
get different benefits,

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:24.800
but that would be a simple
way to think about it.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:29.630
And out in the Mojave Desert,
there's a 350 megawatt

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:34.457
peak output solar
thermal installation.

00:03:34.457 --> 00:03:36.290
This is a little
complicated because they're

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:40.910
using the hot water, which
is steam, to power a turbine.

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:42.767
And that turbine
creates electricity.

00:03:42.767 --> 00:03:44.600
So that's one way of
getting electricity out

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:48.290
of solar thermal, but it's
indirect, unlike photovoltaics.

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:51.030
So that's the distinction.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:53.580
And in terms of developing
world applications,

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:54.830
I hope I have some at the end.

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:57.800
But if not, it's
really common to,

00:03:57.800 --> 00:03:59.960
if you want a hot
shower, to just

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:02.150
have a big bag on your roof.

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:04.970
And it heats up, and then
you get hot water for

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:08.840
as long as that bag lasts, and
then it's cold water again.

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:13.100
So that's one example
that's common.

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:16.709
And then I'll talk about some
PV applications in a bit.

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:22.260
So I wanted to do a quick
overview of photovoltaics.

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:27.980
So basically, again,
the photovoltaic effect

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:32.270
is being taken advantage
of to generate electricity.

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:34.490
This says in silicone.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:36.680
There's a lot of different
photovoltaic materials

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:38.870
I'll touch base on really fast.

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:41.600
There's also confusion
about what this junction is.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:45.530
The junction is
emphasized on the slide

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:49.220
because it's where the
interesting stuff happens

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:49.760
in the cell.

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:55.610
But it's just where the two
slightly different layers

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:58.038
of silicon interact generally.

00:04:58.038 --> 00:05:00.080
There's generally not a
special separate material

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:02.810
there, though this
is complicated

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:05.240
because we're
generalizing, and there's

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:09.230
a lot of different silicon
in photovoltaic materials.

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:10.980
And different ones
behave differently.

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:12.620
But generally--
so I'm just going

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:15.590
to talk about a
traditional silicon cell

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:17.820
because it's very
common and easy.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:22.310
So you have silicon that on the
top side has been doped in such

00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:27.260
a way that's called "n-type."

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:33.050
So it means that it attracts
more negative electrons.

00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:37.020
And the bottom side has
been doped the opposite way,

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:40.010
so called "p-type" for positive.

00:05:40.010 --> 00:05:42.510
So it's attracting more
positive electrons.

00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:44.630
And so you can imagine
if negative, positive,

00:05:44.630 --> 00:05:45.860
you're creating flow.

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:48.470
And that's critical because
you need the flow in order

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:51.840
to get the electricity
out of the cell.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:53.660
So you can see here
you've got your n-type

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:55.520
on top, your p-type on bottom.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:00.470
That's the junction where
the positive electrons

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:01.430
are going down.

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:03.920
The negative electrons
are going up.

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:07.070
And we're getting
electric flow, which

00:06:07.070 --> 00:06:10.035
is providing your electricity
to light your light bulb

00:06:10.035 --> 00:06:10.910
or whatever you want.

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:17.840
So you can think
of this as a diode

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:19.880
because it's allowing
directional flow,

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:22.580
like we talked about a
light-emitting diode.

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:25.250
It's just an
electricity-emitting diode

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:29.470
is one way, maybe,
of thinking about it.

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:31.220
So there's a bunch of
different materials.

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:32.240
Yeah.

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:35.240
STUDENT: So are you saying
the n-type attracts negative,

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:40.320
or it is negative, so
it attracts [INAUDIBLE]..

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:41.295
AMY BANZAERT: So the--

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:46.690
can I remember this--

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:50.230
the n-type has more--

00:06:50.230 --> 00:06:53.780
I can't even remember
because so it's not critical.

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:54.740
There's a difference.

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:55.240
They flow.

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:56.200
Does anyone remember?

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:58.420
STUDENT: The [INAUDIBLE]
is [INAUDIBLE]

00:06:58.420 --> 00:06:59.695
more like those [INAUDIBLE].

00:06:59.695 --> 00:07:00.820
STUDENT: Yeah, [INAUDIBLE].

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:02.590
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE].

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:03.550
AMY BANZAERT: Thanks.

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:04.510
So yeah.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:09.940
So the efficiency
is really going

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:11.770
to vary depending
on the material

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:14.320
and how carefully
it was processed.

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:17.703
And "carefully"
translates to cost.

00:07:17.703 --> 00:07:19.120
And the other thing
to remember is

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:21.910
that it's dependent on light
normal or perpendicular

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:22.730
to the surface.

00:07:22.730 --> 00:07:26.590
So that's why you have to tilt
your solar panels into the sun,

00:07:26.590 --> 00:07:32.860
and why some arrays that
are expensive automatically

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:33.940
tilt themselves.

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:36.160
Other ones you just
put on the house

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:39.580
or wherever you're putting it at
the maximum angle for the most

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:40.580
amount of time.

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:43.930
But if you don't put it in the
right place or the right angle,

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:47.290
you're really going to minimize
the efficacy of the cell.

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:50.590
Someone wanted to know, is
it just silicon that's used?

00:07:50.590 --> 00:07:53.208
No, silicon is most
popular right now.

00:07:53.208 --> 00:07:55.750
But you can see there are some
other materials, some of which

00:07:55.750 --> 00:07:57.167
are more experimental
than others.

00:07:57.167 --> 00:08:00.640
And I'm sure there's additional
examples that I don't even

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:01.900
have on here.

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:04.150
But there's a lot of
different materials used.

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:05.950
All of the pros and
cons, for example--

00:08:05.950 --> 00:08:08.230
cadmium is pretty
toxic if it gets out,

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:09.340
but hopefully it doesn't.

00:08:09.340 --> 00:08:13.550
But it's all challenging.

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:17.710
So how solar-- yeah.

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:19.990
STUDENT: So you use
silicon because it's

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:21.360
a good semiconductor?

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:23.400
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:25.950
Exactly, so silicon is
a good semiconductor.

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:31.770
And a semiconductor means it's
not a full conductor like, say,

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:32.760
a copper wire.

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:36.809
And it's not a full insulator
like, say, the plastic

00:08:36.809 --> 00:08:38.159
around your copper wire.

00:08:38.159 --> 00:08:40.799
It somewhat conducts,
and that allows

00:08:40.799 --> 00:08:47.050
you to do cool things like
having the photovoltaic effect.

00:08:47.050 --> 00:08:49.620
STUDENT: So that wouldn't
work if it was completely

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:51.845
like a conductor?

00:08:51.845 --> 00:08:53.220
AMY BANZAERT:
Complete conductors

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:56.680
don't have a
photovoltaic effect.

00:08:56.680 --> 00:08:58.720
I think it's a good
way to think about it.

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:00.370
Do you agree?

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:03.178
STUDENT: I think you
might be able to make--

00:09:03.178 --> 00:09:05.860
you said [INAUDIBLE],,
but I don't

00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:07.352
you-- you can't [INAUDIBLE].

00:09:07.352 --> 00:09:08.560
AMY BANZAERT: Right, exactly.

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:09.550
So--

00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:11.123
STUDENT: There's no energy gap.

00:09:11.123 --> 00:09:12.790
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah,
all right, thank you

00:09:12.790 --> 00:09:14.080
for helping me with that.

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:16.930
So the clarification is
that the semiconductor

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:21.377
allows you to create flow
so that when you excite

00:09:21.377 --> 00:09:23.710
the electrons in the material,
they have to go somewhere

00:09:23.710 --> 00:09:25.390
in order to be useful.

00:09:25.390 --> 00:09:29.050
And if it's fully conductive,
you can't change--

00:09:29.050 --> 00:09:30.010
how we talked about--

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:35.420
so this is doped
differently than this.

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:37.670
So you have a negative
side or positive side

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:38.660
to allow for flow.

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:40.910
You couldn't do that
in a full conductor.

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:42.603
But you can with
a semiconductor.

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:48.040
Come on, little laptop.

00:09:56.370 --> 00:10:00.810
OK, so photovoltaics
is generally

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:03.170
wiring a bunch of
solar cells together.

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:06.000
You guys wired in
series, I believe,

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:08.290
but they're typically wired
in series or parallel.

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:11.880
And I'll get into
that in just a sec.

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:14.880
And then you can
take those panels

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:19.140
and wire them together
into big, enormous systems.

00:10:22.780 --> 00:10:25.860
And one of the things
that's been challenging

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:28.330
is that they're
sensitive to temperature.

00:10:28.330 --> 00:10:34.350
And so for example, in
areas where it's really hot,

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:36.510
where there's a lot of sun,
that'd be a great place

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:38.490
to put a solar panel
except they get too hot,

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:39.910
and then they get
less efficient.

00:10:39.910 --> 00:10:43.540
So they're really frustrating.

00:10:43.540 --> 00:10:46.590
There were some questions on
series versus parallel wiring.

00:10:46.590 --> 00:10:49.780
And hopefully, this is
just a quick review.

00:10:49.780 --> 00:10:56.310
But if you put two
cells in series,

00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:57.840
the voltage is additive.

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:12.027
And so we have two 6-volt
350 amp-hour cells.

00:11:12.027 --> 00:11:14.110
You put them together and
you're getting 12 volts.

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:16.060
Whereas if you wire
them in parallel,

00:11:16.060 --> 00:11:18.260
the voltage isn't
additive, the current is.

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:21.280
And if you put them in
series and parallel, which

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:23.830
is how they're commonly
wired, then you

00:11:23.830 --> 00:11:28.360
get the voltage and the current
you actually need, ideally.

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:29.980
However, the thing
to always remember

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:33.280
with solar cells, which
hopefully you'll notice today,

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:37.240
is that if you cover
one of the panels up,

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:39.610
that drops the
current in that panel,

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:44.230
and it affects all the panels
when they're wired in series.

00:11:44.230 --> 00:11:47.830
And so it's a really
frustrating reality

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:53.440
that they all need
to be equally clear.

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:56.530
So the components
of a solar system--

00:11:56.530 --> 00:11:59.960
you have a panel, you
have the regulator,

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:06.205
which typically has the
components inside it

00:12:06.205 --> 00:12:08.080
so that you make sure
that the electricity is

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:12.940
flowing in just one
direction, as we talked about.

00:12:12.940 --> 00:12:14.360
That goes to some
sort of storage.

00:12:16.870 --> 00:12:21.460
And then, it goes to an inverter
if you need AC appliances.

00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:26.800
You can see that all of
these different components

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:28.000
have efficiencies.

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:30.790
So every single one is
going to drop your--

00:12:34.390 --> 00:12:36.130
afraid to click too many times--

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:37.810
is going to drop your
efficiency, which

00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:40.430
is really frustrating.

00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:43.900
So again, you have
grid-tied systems

00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:46.930
just like we've had everything
else, or you have standalone,

00:12:46.930 --> 00:12:50.060
or you have hybrid.

00:12:50.060 --> 00:12:56.690
I'm not going to talk
about grid because we don't

00:12:56.690 --> 00:12:58.580
do that much in this class.

00:12:58.580 --> 00:13:02.608
But for standalone systems,
you have your charge controller

00:13:02.608 --> 00:13:04.400
going to your battery
and then an inverter,

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:06.210
so just what we talked about.

00:13:06.210 --> 00:13:14.450
And I don't know why
today is going so poorly,

00:13:14.450 --> 00:13:15.380
but it really is.

00:13:15.380 --> 00:13:16.700
I'm sorry.

00:13:16.700 --> 00:13:17.270
Yeah.

00:13:17.270 --> 00:13:21.410
STUDENT: So for a
cell phone charger,

00:13:21.410 --> 00:13:23.540
you will need not
only some resistance

00:13:23.540 --> 00:13:25.160
to get the total
resistance right,

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:27.970
but you'd also
need that inverter.

00:13:27.970 --> 00:13:30.850
AMY BANZAERT: So
hypothetically, yeah.

00:13:30.850 --> 00:13:33.100
People do all sorts
of bad practices

00:13:33.100 --> 00:13:35.450
and get away with it,
for better or for worse.

00:13:35.450 --> 00:13:39.790
So for example,
the battery charger

00:13:39.790 --> 00:13:44.620
I showed from [INAUDIBLE]
Phoenix on Wednesday

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:49.690
is literally just a panel
with a couple of wires

00:13:49.690 --> 00:13:57.410
coming out, going
to a place where

00:13:57.410 --> 00:13:59.060
you can charge your batteries.

00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:02.990
Which means that it has great
possibility for failure,

00:14:02.990 --> 00:14:05.030
but it's really cheap.

00:14:05.030 --> 00:14:07.430
And so there's always
that trade-off,

00:14:07.430 --> 00:14:13.340
of you can take something
and it'll be super cheap,

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:16.910
and that's handy, but
it won't work very well.

00:14:16.910 --> 00:14:18.800
And you have to figure
out what you want.

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:20.840
You can make this
a $10 million--

00:14:20.840 --> 00:14:26.390
that'd be hard, but $1,000
system or a $1 system,

00:14:26.390 --> 00:14:30.570
and there's real trade-offs in
terms of cost and performance.

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:34.343
STUDENT: But it still
works as direct current?

00:14:34.343 --> 00:14:36.760
AMY BANZAERT: Well, so batteries
only need direct current.

00:14:36.760 --> 00:14:38.310
So they're getting
direct current,

00:14:38.310 --> 00:14:44.380
whereas this computer's
expecting alternating current.

00:14:44.380 --> 00:14:49.710
So I'd need a special device if
I wanted to use direct current.

00:14:49.710 --> 00:14:53.070
Computer's a bad example
because of the Cube

00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:55.950
that actually does take
direct current, too.

00:14:55.950 --> 00:15:01.540
But this projector would
need a special device

00:15:01.540 --> 00:15:03.370
to get direct current.

00:15:07.370 --> 00:15:09.740
And so of course,
you can see extremes

00:15:09.740 --> 00:15:14.990
from a standalone system
in Bangladesh all the way

00:15:14.990 --> 00:15:18.690
to 10 megawatt solar array.

00:15:18.690 --> 00:15:21.035
So this is in Abu Dhabi.

00:15:25.450 --> 00:15:30.220
So I don't want to
take the time to go

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:34.390
through this whole question, but
it'll be in the lecture notes

00:15:34.390 --> 00:15:35.513
that I'll post today.

00:15:35.513 --> 00:15:37.180
And I think I'll post
them in week four,

00:15:37.180 --> 00:15:40.720
because this was the solar
lecture that never was,

00:15:40.720 --> 00:15:43.390
but it makes sense to
put in the solar week.

00:15:43.390 --> 00:15:45.335
But if you can go
through it, this actually

00:15:45.335 --> 00:15:46.210
walks you through it.

00:15:46.210 --> 00:15:48.890
But I recommend doing
it like we do in class.

00:15:48.890 --> 00:15:53.183
So try to do it, and
then if you get stuck,

00:15:53.183 --> 00:15:54.100
you can go through it.

00:15:54.100 --> 00:15:57.970
But this is good practice,
especially because the homework

00:15:57.970 --> 00:15:59.440
has been dying out
in order to make

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:02.210
sure you guys have enough
time to get all the work done.

00:16:02.210 --> 00:16:04.780
But this is a helpful
calculation to be able to do.

00:16:04.780 --> 00:16:07.310
So developing world
applications--

00:16:10.540 --> 00:16:13.300
just quickly, what do you
think people tend to use solar

00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:15.864
for in developing countries?

00:16:15.864 --> 00:16:17.190
STUDENT: Water heaters.

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:20.480
AMY BANZAERT: Water
heaters, what else?

00:16:20.480 --> 00:16:21.338
STUDENT: Light.

00:16:21.338 --> 00:16:22.130
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:16:29.371 --> 00:16:30.538
STUDENT: Cellphone chargers.

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:39.400
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah,
so TV is asking

00:16:39.400 --> 00:16:40.780
a lot out of a solar panel.

00:16:40.780 --> 00:16:43.660
You might see it, but it's
going to be expensive.

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:45.970
And you certainly wouldn't
see a refrigerator on here

00:16:45.970 --> 00:16:47.470
because again,
that's getting really

00:16:47.470 --> 00:16:51.970
expensive in terms of the
size of the panel you need.

00:16:51.970 --> 00:16:53.720
And some examples that
we've talked about,

00:16:53.720 --> 00:16:55.595
but I just wanted to
refresh your memory on--

00:16:55.595 --> 00:17:01.960
so one is Grameen Shakti in
Bangladesh, where women wire up

00:17:01.960 --> 00:17:03.820
the solar panels.

00:17:03.820 --> 00:17:08.619
They use micro-financing to
make the panels affordable.

00:17:08.619 --> 00:17:11.410
They're often in
little markets where

00:17:11.410 --> 00:17:15.220
people buy little amounts of
food and things like that.

00:17:15.220 --> 00:17:16.900
They can also
access electricity,

00:17:16.900 --> 00:17:20.680
or the market can just
have light on at night.

00:17:20.680 --> 00:17:25.030
And they also do
micro-utilities, too.

00:17:25.030 --> 00:17:27.849
I believe there's about 1,000
micro-utilities operating

00:17:27.849 --> 00:17:31.040
in Bangladesh.

00:17:31.040 --> 00:17:33.190
I'm not sure how they
pronounce themselves,

00:17:33.190 --> 00:17:38.050
but this group in Brazil, and
they do a fee-for-service,

00:17:38.050 --> 00:17:39.970
where the company is
responsible for the care

00:17:39.970 --> 00:17:41.260
of the battery,
which is critical

00:17:41.260 --> 00:17:42.670
because the batteries
require care.

00:17:42.670 --> 00:17:44.110
And if they're not
well cared for,

00:17:44.110 --> 00:17:46.630
you can cut their life
by half, if not more.

00:17:46.630 --> 00:17:50.050
And they're a pretty
expensive part of a system.

00:17:50.050 --> 00:17:52.810
They work on a
flexible payment system

00:17:52.810 --> 00:17:54.250
so that the customer decides.

00:17:54.250 --> 00:17:56.530
Because some people
work in areas

00:17:56.530 --> 00:17:59.080
where you might make most
of your money in one month.

00:17:59.080 --> 00:18:00.700
And so if you're
expected to pay month

00:18:00.700 --> 00:18:02.158
by month, that can
be really tough,

00:18:02.158 --> 00:18:05.410
but if you can just pay
once a year, it works.

00:18:05.410 --> 00:18:07.510
And then they do a
lot of user education

00:18:07.510 --> 00:18:12.470
to help make sure people use
them effectively and care

00:18:12.470 --> 00:18:13.730
for them properly.

00:18:13.730 --> 00:18:16.820
SELCO we also talked
about, in India.

00:18:16.820 --> 00:18:18.800
And they have lots
of different examples

00:18:18.800 --> 00:18:23.450
from lighting in a temple,
sorting cocoons for silk

00:18:23.450 --> 00:18:28.460
industry, in houses,
in marketplaces.

00:18:28.460 --> 00:18:30.440
We talked about DLight Design.

00:18:30.440 --> 00:18:33.607
And they have lights that
come with the solar panel

00:18:33.607 --> 00:18:35.690
or have the solar panel
integrated into the light,

00:18:35.690 --> 00:18:38.990
so people have a light
option even if they

00:18:38.990 --> 00:18:40.650
don't have a home installation.

00:18:40.650 --> 00:18:43.340
One of the challenges with
solar like this-- and you'll

00:18:43.340 --> 00:18:44.000
see it a lot.

00:18:44.000 --> 00:18:45.620
People have it on
their bags or you

00:18:45.620 --> 00:18:48.230
can buy a charger or
solar panel that you

00:18:48.230 --> 00:18:51.110
can put wherever-- is it
requires you to be outside

00:18:51.110 --> 00:18:52.610
or it to be outside.

00:18:52.610 --> 00:18:54.290
And so it's challenging.

00:18:54.290 --> 00:18:57.950
I always have a flashlight
that has a solar panel on it.

00:18:57.950 --> 00:18:59.900
And I stopped bringing
it on my field work

00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:03.898
because I'm not necessarily
outside the whole day.

00:19:03.898 --> 00:19:05.690
And I can't just leave
it there, or else it

00:19:05.690 --> 00:19:07.610
will walk if I don't
keep an eye on it.

00:19:07.610 --> 00:19:09.420
And that's true for everyone.

00:19:09.420 --> 00:19:12.230
And so that's one of the
frustrating things about solar

00:19:12.230 --> 00:19:14.810
generally, is that the panel is
expensive and worth something

00:19:14.810 --> 00:19:16.950
whether it's integrated
into the device or not.

00:19:16.950 --> 00:19:20.780
And so figuring out how
you keep the panel secure--

00:19:20.780 --> 00:19:23.240
it's easy to stick a battery
inside a secure room,

00:19:23.240 --> 00:19:26.690
but not so easy to stick a
panel inside a secure room that

00:19:26.690 --> 00:19:28.890
has no roof.

00:19:28.890 --> 00:19:31.640
So that's challenging.

00:19:31.640 --> 00:19:35.060
And they tend to be
small enough that they're

00:19:35.060 --> 00:19:37.192
easier to steal than,
say, like a wind turbine

00:19:37.192 --> 00:19:38.150
or something like that.

00:19:41.510 --> 00:19:44.760
The Solar Thermal Group,
which is an MIT project--

00:19:44.760 --> 00:19:48.710
this is an example of using
solar thermal in a developing

00:19:48.710 --> 00:19:49.520
country.

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:58.190
And they have a wide variety of
applications of different ways

00:19:58.190 --> 00:19:59.660
that they use the solar thermal.

00:19:59.660 --> 00:20:03.230
But some of which I think is
electricity generating as well,

00:20:03.230 --> 00:20:05.840
from the steam.

00:20:05.840 --> 00:20:10.450
So that does have a
real world application.

